※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4552

※ A lambda reads its parameters, what it brought in, and what the whole
※ program has -- and nothing else.  A name from around it that is not in the
※ capture list is one the body may not reach, which is the whole point of the
※ list: what a lambda depends on is written at the top of it rather than found
※ by reading the body.
※
※ C++ says the same with its capture list, and says it for the same reason.
※ What is different here is that there is no way to write "everything": a list
※ that said "whatever the body turns out to use" would be a list that said
※ nothing.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let n: u8 = 1u8
    let f: fn(u8) → u8 = λ a: u8 → u8 { a + n }
    0u6
